<p>In classical taxonomy, Patanjali defines <em>Ishvara</em> not as a creator deity but as a distinct <em>Purusha</em> untouched by <em>klesha</em>, <em>karma</em>, <em>vipaka</em>, or <em>ashaya</em>. Understanding this distinction prevents recurring errors in the metaphysics section. Similarly, the <em>Hatha Yoga Pradipika</em> frames <em>Mitahara</em> as consuming unctuous, sweet food dedicated to Shiva, leaving one-quarter of the stomach empty-a technical definition frequently tested against parallel descriptions in the <em>Gheranda Samhita</em>.</p>
<p>From an anatomical perspective, the mechanics of <em>Pranayama</em> lean heavily on hypercapnic and hypoxic respiratory drives. Slow breathing practices stimulate the vagus nerve, down-regulating sympathetic arousal while optimizing heart rate variability. NTA questions increasingly intersect these biomechanical metrics with traditional yogic nomenclature.</p>
<p>With over 10,000 applicants vying for specialized seats across premier institutions like Banaras Hindu University (BHU), Central University of Rajasthan, and SVYASA, admissions hinge on narrow margins:</p>
